WEBVTT

00:00:00.511 --> 00:00:29.007
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. It's Friday, and that means Wild Spaces and Outdoor Life. Today we've got the Pear Lake burn near Clinton, where the warning window stretches two years past the fire. We're also tracking record ocean heights in California and a Nairobi drainage system built for 2.5 million people, now holding about 5.5 million. And the Deep Dive asks what a place can actually do with months of warning before El Niño arrives. Stay with us.

00:00:42.482 --> 00:01:02.410
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Friday, November 20th, 2026, and today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:10.826
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at a forecast that arrives months before the weather it describes, and at what a place can do with that head start.

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:11.858
<v Riley>Onto the news.

00:01:13.258 --> 00:01:23.354
<v Riley>Most of what's in front of us today is about ground that's moving, water that's rising, and weather that hasn't arrived yet. A good share of it lands close to home.

00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:48.703
<v Riley>The Cariboo Regional District board met for the first time in its new term on Friday, November 13th. Margo Wagner was elected board chair and Tolin Pare vice-chair, both for one-year terms. The board also agreed to send a letter to Rogers Communications about recurring cell and network problems, and Wagner says balancing budgets against property taxes is one of the first focus areas.

00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:52.913
<v Casey>A letter to Rogers is easy. Watch whether the next agenda still mentions it.

00:01:54.513 --> 00:02:13.825
<v Casey>On Sunday, November 8th, NEXT GENeral in Williams Lake ran a free upcycled poppy workshop. The Cariboo Chilcotin Conservation Society's Waste Wise program hosted it, and residents made poppy pins from fabric donated by the Salvation Army thrift store while they shared wartime stories. It was the program's third year, held ahead of Remembrance Day.

00:02:15.297 --> 00:02:40.713
<v Riley>A U.S. congressional representative from Oregon, Maxine Dexter, introduced the Protecting Our Public Lands from Data Centers Act on November 11th. It would ban new data centres on national forests and on land managed by the U.S. Interior Department. Federal land sold or transferred for a data centre without congressional approval would return to government ownership. Thirteen Democrats are cosponsoring the bill.

00:02:41.162 --> 00:02:44.970
<v Casey>The press release names water and wildlife. The permit is what matters.

00:02:46.693 --> 00:02:55.765
<v Casey>The Atlantic reports that demand for salmon is booming, but the people who supply it aren't sure they can keep up. Its headline asks whether cheap salmon is ending.

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:19.865
<v Riley>Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution plans a multiyear study of the SS United States. The retired ocean liner was sunk off Florida on November 11th to become an artificial reef. Initial surveys are planned within a month. Researchers will take water samples at 132 locations and track metals, including iron, manganese, and nickel, as the steel corrodes.

00:03:20.315 --> 00:03:23.547
<v Casey>A month of surveys is easy to fund. Year five is the test.

00:03:25.148 --> 00:03:45.596
<v Casey>University of Otago researchers used drone surveys from 2022 to 2024 to count Erect-crested penguins on the Bounty and Antipodes islands, about 800 kilometres southeast of New Zealand's South Island. One population has been stable for at least 25 years. The other has declined by more than 40 percent over three decades.

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:51.622
<v Riley>Same species, two island groups, opposite trends. That's a better question than the headline.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:04:09.538
<v Riley>The United Nations Environment Programme has published a report warning that the world isn't adapting fast enough to rising climate costs. It says wealthy countries, which produced most of the carbon emissions, have paid out only a fraction of the adaptation money they pledged.

00:04:11.044 --> 00:04:26.836
<v Casey>Popular Mechanics reports that wildfires from decades ago may be thawing permafrost from below in the central Mackenzie Valley, in the Northwest Territories. The result is a new kind of landslide. The article describes the Mackenzie as the largest northward-flowing river in North America.

00:04:27.286 --> 00:04:30.598
<v Riley>Old fire, new slide, and the gap between them is decades.

00:04:32.403 --> 00:04:51.203
<v Riley>A coalition that includes NRDC sued the U.S. government over the third Gulf of Mexico oil and gas lease sale, held under the Trump administration in August. The sale auctioned more than 81.2 million acres of public waters, and the groups say it went ahead without a National Environmental Policy Act review.

00:04:52.728 --> 00:05:05.296
<v Casey>Country Walking reports that mountain rescue call-outs in the UK keep setting records. The piece looks at the numbers, the changing demographics of the people being rescued, and the leading causes, including in England and Wales.

00:05:05.746 --> 00:05:11.114
<v Riley>More people on the hill, or more people in trouble on it. The piece is trying to separate those.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:34.260
<v Riley>MIT Technology Review argues that climate models carry a big blind spot. A synthesis study found that carbon from thawing permafrost, more intense wildfires, and warmer lakes and wetlands could add 0.2 to 0.4 degrees Celsius this century. That's on top of the 2.6 degrees of warming modelers already expect through 2100.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.070
<v Casey>So the number we plan around leaves out the parts that are already moving.

00:05:39.718 --> 00:06:04.798
<v Casey>An internal federal memo from the Canada Water Agency, prepared for Environment Minister Julie Dabrusin, warns that growing pressure on freshwater could harm Canada's long-term security. It says it's unclear whether the country can meet future needs. The memo links water risks to climate change, pollution, population growth, overuse, and aging infrastructure. It came out through an access-to-information request, and some material was withheld.

00:06:06.328 --> 00:06:31.296
<v Riley>An Adafruit blog post covers Simon Willison's project, in which Claude Code built a system called Beep Locator. Ordinary devices, including phones, tablets, and a laptop, joined as listeners and triangulated an intermittent beep to within about 40 centimetres. The culprit was an old carbon monoxide detector with dying batteries. We use Claude ourselves to write this show, so that one's close to home.

00:06:31.562 --> 00:06:31.778
<v Casey>Huh.

00:06:33.486 --> 00:06:43.558
<v Casey>Screwworm was eliminated from the U.S. in 1966. It's back. Scientific American reports that the technique that beat it the first time won't be enough to get rid of it again.

00:06:45.137 --> 00:07:05.233
<v Riley>NASA scientists warn the rapidly intensifying 2026 El Niño could grow larger than the 1997-98 event. They're comparing the two using 35 years of satellite observations. The 1997-98 event brought destructive fires in Indonesia and the Amazon, and crop failures in southern Africa.

00:07:05.683 --> 00:07:11.595
<v Casey>Thirty-five years of satellite data tells you the size of the event. It doesn't tell you where the damage lands.

00:07:13.207 --> 00:07:38.399
<v Casey>Environment Canada escalated rainfall warnings for southwest B.C., with more than 250 millimetres forecast for parts of Vancouver Island on November 10th and 11th. The provincial River Forecast Centre issued a flood watch for much of southwest B.C. Nearly 415,000 BC Hydro customers lost power at some point. In Zeballos, evacuation orders and alerts stayed in place for properties threatened by landslides.

00:07:39.984 --> 00:08:08.576
<v Riley>Alberta introduced Bill 42 on November 18th. For large data centres using at least 10 megawatts, it proposes siting rules, generally at least 600 metres from homes, and a near-zero cooling-water standard. For each new oil and gas well, companies would pay up to $50,000 at the start, then equal annual payments for up to ten years. The province says about 30 per cent of current data-centre proposals could be affected.

00:08:09.026 --> 00:08:16.642
<v Casey>Nathan Ip, Alberta's NDP technology critic, says the water rules don't set a clear standard, and he wants a pause on projects while they're written.

00:08:18.296 --> 00:08:34.736
<v Casey>The U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission approved construction permits on November 9th for four advanced reactors at Dow's Seadrift chemical complex on the Texas Gulf Coast. The permits don't commit Dow to build. Dow expects to consider a preliminary investment decision in 2027.

00:08:35.186 --> 00:08:42.890
<v Riley>Permits come long before any concrete. Lead time is the thread that ties today's weather stories together, and it's where we go next.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:09:03.442
<v Casey>The Cariboo Family Enrichment Centre has offered free family services in 100 Mile House since 1988. That includes counselling, prenatal support, childcare resource and referral, and the Raven Youth Activity Centre for teens. If you're a parent, a grandparent, or a teenager with nowhere in town to go after school, check out cariboofamily.org.

00:09:04.084 --> 00:09:10.508
<v Riley>A free service in a community of about 1,900 people is the kind of thing you only learn about when you need it.

00:09:11.908 --> 00:09:37.636
<v Riley>The World Meteorological Organization declared the onset of El Niño in early June. It began strengthening in August, and it's expected to reach record strength by December, with heat and disruptive rain running into 2027. That is a long runway. So when a warning arrives months before the damage, what can a place actually do with the lead time, and what does a global forecast leave each place to work out alone?

00:09:37.978 --> 00:09:50.506
<v Casey>A loud warning isn't a prepared place. The WMO's numbers are global averages. Temperatures from January through September ran about 1.47 degrees above preindustrial levels. An average doesn't tell a town which culvert fails.

00:09:51.044 --> 00:10:16.108
<v Riley>Lead time comes from somewhere, though. Satellite records and seasonal models are why we knew in June what was coming in December. The Niño 3.4 index climbed from about 1.5 degrees above normal in May through July to 2.0 in July. The WHO has classed the expected health effects as an emergency, the first time it has done that for an El Niño. It also published a Grade 3 preparedness plan.

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:35.695
<v Casey>A plan isn't a result. WMO's September update put the chance that El Niño persists through February 2027 near 100 percent. The record label is the uncertain part. The U.S. Climate Prediction Center gives roughly 60 percent odds on a record-strength season. Sixty percent is a bet you wouldn't take with a highway.

00:10:35.695 --> 00:10:43.943
<v Riley>Sixty percent is still a forecast you can plan around. You don't wait for certainty to fund a drainage fix. You size it for the range.

00:10:44.576 --> 00:10:53.200
<v Casey>Sized for which end of the range? The WMO's secretary-general called the situation "off the charts." A culvert needs a diameter, and a diameter needs a number.

00:10:53.844 --> 00:11:19.380
<v Riley>Then take the numbers that exist. Nairobi has one. Bramwell Simiyu, the disaster-management chief for Nairobi County, says the drainage system was designed 30 years ago for 2.5 million people. The city now holds about 5.5 million at night. He puts a full sewerage and drainage upgrade at up to $192 million. The forecast doesn't lower that bill. It only changes how soon someone has to start paying it.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:27.992
<v Casey>A warning doesn't build a sewer. Months of lead time won't turn $192 million into a line in a budget that survives the next election.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:52.050
<v Riley>Sewers aren't a warning's job, though. Ghana's gap is cheaper. Professor Mawuli Dzodzomenyo, at the University of Ghana School of Public Health, says the country has weak heat surveillance and few response protocols. He calls for early-warning systems so health services can prepare for heat illness before the season arrives. That's a protocol. It doesn't need a new pipe in the ground.

00:11:52.604 --> 00:12:02.644
<v Casey>Protocols are cheap to write and expensive to keep running. Who staffs the heat alert in year five? If the answer is a volunteer with a binder, the protocol dies in the first quiet season.

00:12:03.171 --> 00:12:16.139
<v Riley>Then put it inside an existing health office. Make the heat alert a line in someone's job description, and rehearse it before the season, not during it. The cost is staff hours, and those are the cheapest part of this.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:24.844
<v Casey>Cheap to say. Who checks the alert reaches the clinic on the far side of the river? Notices fail most often where nobody checked who was on the list.

00:12:25.527 --> 00:12:34.343
<v Riley>Write that into the protocol. Name the clinic. Name the nursing station. Call each one once in a dry month and see whether the number still works.

00:12:34.843 --> 00:12:55.779
<v Casey>Fine. Now California, where the warning already has a tide gauge attached. Scientific American reports ocean waters off the state are at record heights. Waves above 50 feet hit San Mateo County over the weekend. Seawater filled yards in Mission Beach in San Diego, and a wave overtopped a berm at Seal Beach. The King Tides are still to come, on top of a strong El Niño.

00:12:56.343 --> 00:13:19.559
<v Riley>The weekend hits came before the main event. NOAA's Office for Coastal Management recorded all-time high water levels at four California coastal sites in late October, and Los Angeles Harbor logged its highest tide ever. The drivers were El Niño-fuelled Kelvin waves, a perigean spring tide, and an ongoing marine heatwave. The gauges caught it early. That's monitoring doing its job.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:40.844
<v Casey>Caught it, and it's still coming through the door. A gauge reading doesn't move a highway. The Los Angeles Times describes the combination as moderately high astronomical tides plus record-breaking increases in the ocean itself, and says the ocean rise is now the larger effect. The tide table is what every coastal plan is built on, and the tide table doesn't include that.

00:13:40.844 --> 00:14:03.508
<v Riley>Which is a reason to fix the model, not to drop the gauges. The forecasts for the coming King Tides say they could run half a foot to a foot above the published tables, with the next set on November 24th to 26th. NOAA predicts 7.02 feet at Port San Luis on December 24th, which could top that gauge's record. A planner can use those numbers.

00:14:04.177 --> 00:14:17.993
<v Casey>Only if someone plans against it. The Los Angeles Times says flooding is quickly becoming a fact of life in some Southern California coastal communities. That's what it sounds like when people stop waiting for relief and start adjusting to the water.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:30.022
<v Riley>A warning only helps the assets you can move or harden in time. A highway can't move. A pump station can be raised, and a treatment plant can be given a wall. Which of those are already on a list?

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:44.726
<v Casey>Hardening a highway is a capital project with a contract, a consultant, and a string of approvals. Months of lead time don't speed up a contract that started years ago, and the Scientific American piece names highways and wastewater plants as the exposed assets.

00:14:45.343 --> 00:14:53.711
<v Riley>Which argues for starting the contract before the warning, not after. Lead time only helps the places that already have the first step underway.

00:14:54.322 --> 00:14:58.946
<v Casey>In a lot of places the honest answer is none. Nobody wants that answer in a press release.

00:14:59.846 --> 00:15:27.790
<v Riley>Our own version is smaller, and closer. We covered the Pear Lake wildfire analysis near Clinton last Friday, and the part that matters here is the window. The Thompson-Nicola Regional District says the risk is highest in the two years after a fire. Moderate to high burn severity across the watersheds raises runoff, and the hazard is expected to stay elevated for one to two years. That's a warning attached to a named village with a named time window.

00:15:28.443 --> 00:15:36.595
<v Casey>The two-year window is the important part. A flood watch lasts days. Burned slopes carry the risk for years, and the public notice doesn't last that long.

00:15:37.141 --> 00:15:51.445
<v Riley>Then the warning has to be attached to something permanent. A creek crossing, a road, a slope with a number. If the mapping already exists, tying a forecast to a named crossing is cheap work, a short list of places and not a new agency.

00:15:52.112 --> 00:16:02.320
<v Casey>If the mapping exists. Who keeps the map current when the burn is five years old and the rain comes back? A map from the first season is a snapshot, and a snapshot goes stale first.

00:16:02.849 --> 00:16:13.857
<v Riley>Name the owner and fund the update. Put the update in the same line as the crew that inspects the road. That line has to survive the next budget cycle, and that's the design problem.

00:16:14.427 --> 00:16:23.651
<v Casey>Budget lines get cut, and nobody's name is on the next one. I'd rather see a name on the map than a line in the budget. A line can be reallocated by a single motion.

00:16:24.202 --> 00:16:32.514
<v Riley>A name on the map is only as good as the crew behind it. I'd take both, and I'd start with the map, since the map takes a weekend to draft.

00:16:33.186 --> 00:16:42.594
<v Casey>Then who's in the room? The people who use the land see the slope change first. They know which road floods before the sign goes up. They're rarely the ones writing the warning.

00:16:43.221 --> 00:16:58.997
<v Riley>So the landing matters. The forecasting agency gives the regional picture, and it's good at that. The regional government can turn the picture into a named creek, and it already holds the maps and the budget. The people who use the land know which road to avoid.

00:16:59.655 --> 00:17:10.471
<v Casey>If each of them assumes the other two will do the turning, the warning becomes a press release with a lead time nobody spent. That's the failure I'd bet on, because it doesn't show up in any single report.

00:17:10.984 --> 00:17:31.152
<v Riley>I'd give the turning to the regional government, and bring the land users in from the first meeting, not the third. The lead time is real, and the places that use it are the ones that already started. If you have a story tip or a creek you think belongs on the map, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Enjoy your weekend.

00:17:31.725 --> 00:17:43.925
<v Casey>I still don't know who's in that room in year five. The forecast can say a season will be extreme, but not what it does to a particular creek, road, or slope that burned two summers ago. Check the creek before you cross it.

00:17:44.525 --> 00:18:01.725
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.